### Review of Step 1 (`TCPMessage.java`)

You nailed the essential fields! Having `id`, `methodName`, `args`, `result`, and `exception` in [Server.Interface](file:///C:/Users/josep/OneDrive%20-%20McGill%20University/Desktop/Semester%209/COMP512/PA1-Group47/Template/Server/Server/Interface/TCPMessage.java) is the right structure.

There are **two critical things** to add before it will work over a network:
1. **`implements java.io.Serializable`**: Network sockets send raw bytes. Java requires classes sent over `ObjectOutputStream` to implement `Serializable`, otherwise it throws `NotSerializableException`.
2. **Constructors and Getters/Setters**: So the client can create a request, and the server can inspect the method name, read the arguments, and set the result.

Here is how you should complete [TCPMessage.java](file:///C:/Users/josep/OneDrive%20-%20McGill%20University/Desktop/Semester%209/COMP512/PA1-Group47/Template/Server/Server/Interface/TCPMessage.java):

```java
package Server.Interface;

import java.io.Serializable;
import java.util.UUID;

public class TCPMessage implements Serializable {
    private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L;

    private String id;
    private String methodName;
    private Object[] args;
    private Object result;
    private Exception exception;

    // Constructor for creating a REQUEST (called by the Client/Proxy)
    public TCPMessage(String methodName, Object... args) {
        this.id = UUID.randomUUID().toString();
        this.methodName = methodName;
        this.args = args;
    }

    // Constructor for creating a RESPONSE (called by Server/Middleware)
    public TCPMessage(String id, Object result, Exception exception) {
        this.id = id;
        this.result = result;
        this.exception = exception;
    }

    // Getters and Setters
    public String getId() { return id; }
    public String getMethodName() { return methodName; }
    public Object[] getArgs() { return args; }
    public Object getResult() { return result; }
    public void setResult(Object result) { this.result = result; }
    public Exception getException() { return exception; }
    public void setException(Exception exception) { this.exception = exception; }
}
```

